
  Move a subset of a worktree's uncommitted changes to another worktree
  (new or existing) to split an oversized PR or isolate unrelated work.

  On a terminal the wizard asks what the flags leave open: the source worktree,
  the files, the target (an existing worktree, or a new one: its branch, source
  branch, env strategy and isolation), move or copy, and what to do when the
  files conflict with the target; its recap previews the plan, then applies
  exactly that plan.
  With --yes, --output json or no terminal nothing is asked: a source, --files
  and --to are then required.

  A --to branch that already exists locally is checked out as-is, keeping its
  commits. Its parent can't be inferred, so --from then names the branch recorded
  for `wtm sync` — asked in the wizard, required without it.

  Untracked files are listed one by one, including inside brand-new directories,
  so you can take part of a new folder; gitignored files are never listed.

  Conflicts are found before anything is created or moved. It aborts by default,
  leaving both worktrees untouched; --on-conflict resolve writes conflict markers
  in the target so you can resolve them like a rebase. A file that merely already
  exists in the target with other content counts as a conflict too.
  --dry-run shows the plan, its conflicts and a fingerprint.

  Usage:
    wtm-next extract [source] [flags]

  Examples:
    # Pick the source, the files and the target
    wtm extract

    # Move a directory's changes to a new branch
    wtm extract feat/login --files apps/api --to feat/login-api --yes

    # Copy one file instead, onto a branch stacked on the source
    wtm extract feat/login --files apps/web/login.ts --to feat/login-web --from feat/login --keep --yes

  Flags:
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --env-from string             How the .env files of the new target are provisioned: config (config.toml's strategy), example (from its template), main (copied from the main checkout) or parent (from the worktree of the branch it starts from, --from); defaults to config.toml's strategy
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
        --ff                          Fast-forward the parent branch to origin before creating the target; skipped when it has diverged
        --files strings               Files to extract, or a directory to take everything below it
        --from string                 Parent branch when creating the target worktree
    -h, --help                        help for extract
        --isolation string            How the new worktree stands against its source: isolated (its own ports, compose project and namespaces in shared services, in the .env and at run time) or verbatim (.env kept exactly as copied, run on its source's ports and data); defaults to run.toml's isolation, else isolated
        --keep                        Copy instead of move (keep the changes in the source)
        --on-conflict string          On conflict: abort (default) or resolve (write conflict markers in the target)
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
        --remember stringArray        The answers to always use in this repository: env_from, isolation, fast_forward (repeatable)
        --to string                   Target worktree branch; created if it does not exist
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation; what is not given takes its default (a source, --files and --to are required, and --from when --to already exists locally)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

